I did not start `git-subtrees` because I wanted to invent another way to vendor a library. I started it because I wanted two ways of working at once.

Inside my monorepo, I wanted every project to behave like an ordinary directory. One branch, one checkout and one atomic commit should be enough to change a command-line tool together with the library or configuration it depends on. Outside the monorepo, selected directories should remain useful as independent repositories.

Most importantly, the branch names should travel across that boundary:

```text
monorepo: feature/better-imports
             │
             ├── tools/parser: feature/better-imports
             └── libs/model:   feature/better-imports
```

If a directory changed, pushing the monorepo branch should create or update the same-named branch in its upstream repository. A directory that did not change should not gain a pointless branch.

## Why not git-subrepo?

[`git-subrepo`](https://github.com/ingydotnet/git-subrepo) solves a related problem: it puts a repository into a normal directory and supports changes in both directions. Its state file, however, records one upstream branch. Switching the monorepo from `main` to `feature/better-imports` does not make the embedded repository follow that branch. A command can override the configured branch, but the model remains “this directory tracks that branch.”

My model was different: **the current monorepo branch determines the upstream branch**. The default branches correspond even when one repository still calls its default `master`; every other branch keeps its name.

That difference reaches far beyond command syntax. A useful status command must answer, for every directory:

- Does the same-named upstream branch exist?
- Is the directory ahead, behind or diverged?
- If the branch does not exist, did this directory actually change relative to the base branch?
- Which repositories should a coordinated push touch?

`git-subrepo` was not a poor tool. Its fixed branch mapping was a poor fit for the workflow I wanted.

## Why not submodules?

Submodules keep repository boundaries honest, but they also keep them visible during daily work. The parent repository stores a pointer; the directory is another checkout with its own branch state. After cloning or switching the parent branch, the submodule commonly ends up on a detached `HEAD`.

Hooks can switch every submodule to the matching branch, and `push.recurseSubmodules` can coordinate pushes. But then the seamless monorepo workflow depends on hooks continually repairing the fact that I am operating several repositories.

I wanted the opposite trade-off: **one working tree by default, repository boundaries only when synchronizing**.

## The first design

Git already had the hard transformation I needed. `git subtree split` can rebuild the history of one directory as though that directory had always been the repository root. `git subtree pull --squash` can bring an upstream version back without importing every upstream commit.

So `git-subtrees` began as orchestration around `git subtree`:

1. name each Git remote after its directory;
2. map the current monorepo branch to the same upstream branch;
3. inspect every directory and classify its state;
4. pull or push only where necessary;
5. create a missing upstream branch only for a directory that changed.

That worked well enough to expose the next layer of problems. A remote named after a directory looked elegant, but it still represented a whole repository. Synchronization state hidden in merge history worked, until that history was rewritten. Splitting history on every status check was exact, but increasingly slow.

Those were not unrelated bugs. They were signs that I had reused Git concepts at the wrong boundaries. The rest of this series is about following those signs until `git subtree` disappeared from the design—and `git-subtrees` became Git Splice.
